---
name: edit-with-purpose
description: Clean up, proofread or edit existing prose through targeted changes that preserve effective wording and the author's voice. Use for an editorial pass or independent review.
metadata:
  version: "0.3.0-alpha.5"
---

Improve the author's existing prose through targeted changes. The user's explicit request takes priority over this guidance. Start from the supplied wording and preserve effective language, precise terms and the author's voice. When the user identifies bloat or abstract phrasing, revise the affected sentences enough to resolve it; preserving the criticized wording is not successful restraint. Distinguish original wording, approved style references, factual sources and rejected generated text; a rejected draft is not a voice sample. Follow an explicit request for a substantial rewrite when one is given.

For a requested independent pass, use a fresh agent context when available and authorized. Supply the draft, relevant brief and author constraints, and this skill. An agent already assigned that fresh context performs the review directly. Keep the writer's rationale and previous review verdicts outside the first reading. State a limitation if a fresh context is unavailable; a separate skill name alone establishes no independence.

Read the whole passage in context before changing its parts. Recover the point, reasoning and intended action. Identify a particular problem in the original: an unclear referent or connection, repeated work, unnecessary explanation, an error, or a break in the paragraph's flow. A generic desire to polish the text is insufficient reason to replace effective wording.

For Cole Lyons's prose, read [Cole's approved writing style](references/cole-lyons-style.md) before editing, unless the current request calls for a different voice. After style feedback, compare the rejected wording with the author's preferred version before editing again. Use the differences to guide word choice, context, connections and sentence boundaries. A requested style match can justify replacing wording that is grammatical but conflicts with the preferred sample. Apply the preference to the phrasing itself; copying transition words or joining existing clauses alone is not a style match. Read [Learning from a preferred rewrite](references/plain-language-edit.md#learn-from-a-preferred-rewrite) for a worked comparison. A lower word count or shorter sentences alone does not establish easier reading.

Work from the original with the smallest set of changes that resolves the requested problem. This may be a word, a sentence, or coordinated changes to a paragraph. A small, justified edit may add a word when that makes the intended claim more precise; explicit length limits still apply. Retain unaffected language and use the existing progression when it works. A correction-only request does not invite a new structure; a request to cut bloat or explain plainly permits the deletions and sentence revisions needed to do so. Make a substantial change in organization explicit.

For concision, remove repeated meaning while retaining context and connecting words the reader needs. Unless the task requires complete coverage, a list is a selection of examples: retain the examples needed to establish the point, not every item merely because it appeared in the draft. Replace an inventory with a shared role only when that role is accurate. For abstract or figurative phrasing, state the supported actor, action and consequence in ordinary language. Do not substitute a new metaphor or omit a material condition to sound simpler. Keep deliberate imagery when it serves the requested effect; a plain-language request can override it. For list-heavy or abstract prose, or a qualifier that changes a claim's scope, read [Plain-language editing](references/plain-language-edit.md).

Make the chosen changes one at a time and reread their connections in the complete passage. This is deliberate editing, not artificial delay or a request for permission after each sentence. Count the final text when the brief sets a word ceiling or says not to expand. Use the original word count as the ceiling only when the request requires no growth; do not invent a percentage reduction from a general request to tighten. Meet an explicit limit before claiming completion, or explain the unresolved conflict with required content. Use an available counter; the optional writer checker supports declared limits as described in [Mechanical checks](references/mechanical-checks.md). A successful count does not establish good editing.

For saved UTF-8 text revisions, use [Local edits and change measurement](references/local-edits.md) to apply named changes to a source snapshot and produce a separate candidate. If that tool is unavailable, preserve the local editing method and identify the missing mechanical verification. Count before and after, words inserted and removed, and how much original wording was selected for replacement. These observations expose expansion and broad rewriting; they do not score authenticity or usefulness.

Check meaning separately. When sources are supplied in stages, complete the first reader edit before opening them. Compare the revised claims with the relevant evidence, preserving necessary attribution, uncertainty, conditions, chronology and quantitative relationships. Distinguish an omitted detail from a misleading omission. Correct a substantive error when the source supports the correction; identify consequential unresolved questions. Report the scope actually checked: source fidelity alone does not establish factual truth. Missing sources leave factual validation unavailable.

Consult a resource only for the current issue:

- For a contested concept, assertion state, value binding or obligation, read [Technical meaning](references/technical-meaning.md).
- For requested exact observations, read [Mechanical checks](references/mechanical-checks.md). The existing writer's checker is optional support, not a dependency for editing.

Deliver the edited text and only material change notes the task needs. Preserve the original when editing a file unless authorized to replace it. Show targeted changes or their measurements when requested; ordinary replies need no process log. When assessment is requested, report conformance, correctness and usefulness separately. Complete the editorial pass, its meaning check and a final reading for flow. Use the author's response to assess style fit; mechanical checks and the editor's own preference do not establish acceptance. Check the result against the user's specific criticism: each named list or phrase should be addressed, or its retention explained. Further work needs a particular unresolved issue or the user's request, rather than a quota of changes or an automatic rewriting loop.
